# 2026-03-25 — TIL: Promise.any returns the first fulfilled promise (and fails if all reject)

Promise.any(iterable) resolves with the first promise that fulfills. If every promise rejects, it rejects with an AggregateError containing all rejection reasons.

Example:
```js
const p1 = Promise.reject('p1 err');
const p2 = new Promise(r => setTimeout(() => r('p2 ok'), 50));
const p3 = new Promise((_, rej) => setTimeout(() => rej('p3 err'), 10));

Promise.any([p1, p2, p3])
  .then(value => console.log('fulfilled:', value)) // -> "fulfilled: p2 ok"
  .catch(err => console.error('all rejected:', err)); // AggregateError if all reject
```

Notes:
- Unlike Promise.race, Promise.any ignores rejections until no fulfillments remain.
- Use AggregateError.errors to inspect individual rejection reasons (supported in modern runtimes).
- Polyfill: core-js or a small wrapper using Promise.allSettled to simulate behavior in older environments.